---
The toolchain's own test support: an in-memory store whose reads are typed, so
the compiler's call-site decode of a first-party capability reply (#3458) is
exercised end to end. It is experimental and not part of the language surface:
no program is expected to depend on it, and it may change or go in any release.
---
adapter bynk.testing {
	exports capability { TypedReads }

	---
	An in-memory store of JSON values. `write` stores the boundary encoding of
	its value, so `read` and `readOpt` decode what a call put there: a value
	stored as one type and read as another does not decode.
	---
	capability TypedReads {
		---
		Store the JSON text `json` under `key`, as it is, so a test can seed a
		value no type would have written.
		---
		fn put(key: String, json: String) -> Effect[()]
		---
		Store the encoding of `value` under `key`.
		---
		fn write[T: Codable](key: String, value: T) -> Effect[()]
		---
		The value under `key`, decoded as `T`. A missing key, and a value that
		does not decode as `T`, fault.
		---
		fn read[T: Codable](key: String) -> Effect[T]
		---
		The value under `key`, decoded as `T`. A missing key, and a value that
		does not decode as `T`, are `None`: the value is read as a miss, and a
		warning goes to the runtime console.
		---
		fn readOpt[T: Codable](key: String) -> Effect[Option[T]] @onUndecodable(miss)
	}

	provides TypedReads = MemoryTypedReads
}
